# Coil — Maze

This file fixes the mode the game is played in, the menu entry that starts it,
and the course of obstacles laid across the board. The board it is played on is
in `specs/board.md`, and everything the tick resolves is in `specs/movement.md`.
Every figure below carries the name this specification gives it.

## The mode

The game ships one mode, Maze, and every round is a round of it. It is the grid
serpent played on the enclosed `GRID_COLS x GRID_ROWS` board of
`specs/board.md`, with a fixed course of obstacle cells laid across the interior.
The border is solid and fatal, the obstacles are solid and fatal, and exactly one
pellet is on the board at a time.

Maze takes the tick, the turning rules, the growth rule, and the collision rules
of `specs/movement.md`, the pellet placement of `specs/board.md`, and the scoring
of `specs/scoring.md` exactly as those files state them. The obstacle course
below is its one addition.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Menu entry | `MODE_ITEM` | `MAZE` |
| HUD label | `MODE_LABEL` | `MAZE` |

`MODE_ITEM` is the first item of the title menu, above `HOW TO PLAY`, as
`specs/ui.md` states. `MODE_LABEL` is the mode readout in the HUD.

## The obstacle course

`OBSTACLE_CELLS` holds the obstacle cells. They are the same cells in every
round, they never change while a round runs, and they are exactly these four
bars.

| Bar | Cells `(col, row)` |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | `(8,4)`, `(9,4)`, `(10,4)`, `(11,4)`, `(12,4)`, `(13,4)` |
| 2 | `(16,13)`, `(17,13)`, `(18,13)`, `(19,13)`, `(20,13)`, `(21,13)` |
| 3 | `(8,10)`, `(8,11)`, `(8,12)` |
| 4 | `(21,5)`, `(21,6)`, `(21,7)` |

That is 18 cells. Row `8`, the row the snake starts on, carries none of them, so
the opening chain has a clear runway ahead of it.

The course obeys three rules.

- An obstacle cell is fatal to the head, exactly as a wall cell is. Step 3 of the
  tick ends the round when the new head cell is one of them.
- An obstacle cell is never a valid pellet cell, so a pellet never spawns on the
  course. It counts against the valid set that decides the board-cleared win.
- An obstacle cell is part of the board rather than part of the snake. It is
  drawn one cell in size, distinctly from the wall border, from the snake, and
  from the pellet, so a player reads the course at a glance.
